00:04:45.220 --> 00:04:49.630
So Google Cloud, it's part of
the information superhighway.

00:04:49.630 --> 00:04:52.210
And a fair and
reasonable criticism

00:04:52.210 --> 00:04:55.350
that Google has had
to withstand is, hey,

00:04:55.350 --> 00:04:57.190
your services are fantastic.

00:04:57.190 --> 00:04:58.840
That's the information
superhighway.

00:04:58.840 --> 00:05:02.130
But would you please
pave the on ramps?

00:05:02.130 --> 00:05:04.440
And so that's what we're
discussing here today

00:05:04.440 --> 00:05:07.530
with the announcement of
the data warehouse migration

00:05:07.530 --> 00:05:08.280
service.

00:05:08.280 --> 00:05:11.130
So this is an agent,
or a tool, that you

00:05:11.130 --> 00:05:15.720
can use to automatically move
data and schema from OnPrem

00:05:15.720 --> 00:05:20.430
to BigQuery and not have
to manage that process

00:05:20.430 --> 00:05:22.890
or not have to manage the
details of that transformation

00:05:22.890 --> 00:05:27.420
in the schema migration by hand.

00:05:27.420 --> 00:05:31.350
The way it works is, at
the top left box here,

00:05:31.350 --> 00:05:34.020
is that the source,
the OnPrem system.

00:05:34.020 --> 00:05:37.680
You actually download an agent
onto a VM in your source system

00:05:37.680 --> 00:05:39.360
that then configures
the migration.

00:05:39.360 --> 00:05:44.160
It looks into your tables,
the metadata in your tables,

00:05:44.160 --> 00:05:46.770
and figures out what
the relationships are

00:05:46.770 --> 00:05:49.960
and then posts that
back to GCP and says,

00:05:49.960 --> 00:05:52.630
OK, I understand what the schema
should be inside of BigQuery.

00:05:52.630 --> 00:05:53.730
Let's move it.

00:05:53.730 --> 00:05:55.530
And then it transfers
the data over

00:05:55.530 --> 00:05:59.220
into BigQuery using the
BigQuery data transfer service.

00:05:59.220 --> 00:06:01.380
And then loads that
straight into BigQuery

00:06:01.380 --> 00:06:02.940
as the same schema--

00:06:02.940 --> 00:06:05.910
and it transforms data types,
et cetera, as the same schema

00:06:05.910 --> 00:06:08.400
that you started
with in Teradata.

00:06:08.400 --> 00:06:10.500
So we are very excited
about this tool.

00:06:10.500 --> 00:06:11.810
We want our partners to use it.

00:06:11.810 --> 00:06:14.280
We want our customers
to use it to make

00:06:14.280 --> 00:06:16.890
it a lot easier to get their
data from the OnPrem system

00:06:16.890 --> 00:06:18.240
to the new one.

00:06:18.240 --> 00:06:22.290
Of note, we should say that
the supported sources right now

00:06:22.290 --> 00:06:24.662
are Teradata, Redshift, and S3.

00:06:24.662 --> 00:06:26.370
And we're going to be
expanding this list

00:06:26.370 --> 00:06:29.870
as the beta continues.

00:06:29.870 --> 00:06:33.380
We also have some announcements
around individual features.

00:06:33.380 --> 00:06:36.508
So these were contained
in the keynote.

00:06:36.508 --> 00:06:38.300
But let's just spend
a moment on them here.

00:06:38.300 --> 00:06:40.220
They're relevant for
data warehouse migrations

00:06:40.220 --> 00:06:42.170
because, if you
move, say, Teradata

00:06:42.170 --> 00:06:45.230
to BigQuery but BigQuery
doesn't have all the features

00:06:45.230 --> 00:06:46.880
that you're accustomed
to it Teradata,

00:06:46.880 --> 00:06:49.430
then it won't be the
same rich experience

00:06:49.430 --> 00:06:52.490
as you're accustomed to, like
with the brakes taken off.

00:06:52.490 --> 00:06:54.530
So we are very
aware that we need

00:06:54.530 --> 00:06:56.090
to make sure that
you have things

00:06:56.090 --> 00:06:59.810
like integer range partitioning,
scripts and stored procedures.

00:06:59.810 --> 00:07:02.460
Those are all in
alpha right now.

00:07:02.460 --> 00:07:06.530
The BigQuery storage API is
another very significant launch

00:07:06.530 --> 00:07:07.730
that's gone to beta.

00:07:07.730 --> 00:07:10.910
And that actually allows
either you or any analysis tool

00:07:10.910 --> 00:07:13.520
to get direct access
to BigQuery storage

00:07:13.520 --> 00:07:17.900
in an exceedingly performant way
in an unlimited way, as well.

00:07:17.900 --> 00:07:21.000
Right now, export jobs are
limited to 10 terabytes per day

00:07:21.000 --> 00:07:21.500
with.

00:07:21.500 --> 00:07:24.450
The storage API, you can extract
as much data as you need.

00:07:24.450 --> 00:07:27.410
So this speeds up things like
visualization tools or data

00:07:27.410 --> 00:07:29.660
flow and Dataproc
jobs that are running

00:07:29.660 --> 00:07:31.400
on data stored in BigQuery.

00:07:31.400 --> 00:07:33.560
The goal of that
API, actually, is

00:07:33.560 --> 00:07:35.990
to make it so that
BigQuery is the place

00:07:35.990 --> 00:07:38.510
to store all your structured
data because you can get

00:07:38.510 --> 00:07:41.120
to it with any analysis tool
you want to once it's inside

00:07:41.120 --> 00:07:42.530
of BigQuery.

00:07:42.530 --> 00:07:45.200
And then launching to general
availability, clustering

00:07:45.200 --> 00:07:48.440
is going GA, when GA
yesterday, BigQuery GIS,

00:07:48.440 --> 00:07:50.240
and the MERGE statement.

00:07:50.240 --> 00:07:53.720
So you can see by our efforts
here that we are continually

00:07:53.720 --> 00:07:57.230
improving BigQuery so that, when
you migrate your data from one

00:07:57.230 --> 00:08:00.110
to the next, that you have
all the features and all

00:08:00.110 --> 00:08:05.280
of the speed and velocity
that you need in the cloud.

00:08:05.280 --> 00:08:07.350
One of the things that I
think the BigQuery team

00:08:07.350 --> 00:08:09.570
does particularly well--
and yes, I'm biased.

00:08:09.570 --> 00:08:11.520
But I'm going to say it anyway--

00:08:11.520 --> 00:08:15.480
is that we try to engage very,
very deeply with our customers.

00:08:15.480 --> 00:08:18.540
At scale, we have a
public issue tracker.

00:08:18.540 --> 00:08:22.620
And we track issues that
the public reports to us.

00:08:22.620 --> 00:08:24.750
And so upvotes in the
public issue tracker

00:08:24.750 --> 00:08:26.590
does move things
up in our roadmap.

00:08:26.590 --> 00:08:29.400
So I encourage you all to use
that and upvote the features

00:08:29.400 --> 00:08:31.890
that you want us to make next.

00:08:31.890 --> 00:08:35.370
For platinum
customers like HSBC,

00:08:35.370 --> 00:08:38.760
we actually get into the
weeds and design our betas

00:08:38.760 --> 00:08:42.690
and GA requirements
the requirements

00:08:42.690 --> 00:08:44.159
that our customers have.

00:08:44.159 --> 00:08:46.080
I won't read through
all of these features

00:08:46.080 --> 00:08:49.170
that HSBC is using
inside of BigQuery.

00:08:49.170 --> 00:08:52.110
But I'll point to the
one on the bottom right.

00:08:52.110 --> 00:08:53.850
So customer managed
encryption keys--

00:08:53.850 --> 00:08:56.580
we actually designed the
general availability launch

00:08:56.580 --> 00:09:03.630
requirements to match what HSBC
needed so that we could get

00:09:03.630 --> 00:09:06.400
to GA faster for the use case
that we needed to support,

00:09:06.400 --> 00:09:10.000
which was to please this
very important customer.

00:09:10.000 --> 00:09:13.030
In any case, so
with that, I'm going

00:09:13.030 --> 00:09:15.790
to kind of tie the bow in
the intro and announcements.

00:09:15.790 --> 00:09:18.600
And talking about the
features that HSBC is using

00:09:18.600 --> 00:09:22.732
