00:09:33.600 --> 00:09:36.090
So my name is Srinivas Vaddadi.

00:09:36.090 --> 00:09:39.570
And I'm a delivery
manager at HSBC.

00:09:39.570 --> 00:09:43.530
So I lead the data
on cloud program.

00:09:43.530 --> 00:09:48.780
And my team supports the
cloud migration initiatives.

00:09:48.780 --> 00:09:53.347
So I just want to start
with a quick intro on HSBC.

00:09:53.347 --> 00:09:54.930
I think many of you
already know HSBC.

00:09:54.930 --> 00:09:57.540
But just to give you
some numbers here,

00:09:57.540 --> 00:10:00.720
we have it on 39
million customers

00:10:00.720 --> 00:10:03.720
spread across 66 countries.

00:10:03.720 --> 00:10:05.400
So coming to the
data assets spot,

00:10:05.400 --> 00:10:07.320
we have been growing rapidly.

00:10:07.320 --> 00:10:12.870
And as of 2018, we have it
on 169 petabytes of data.

00:10:12.870 --> 00:10:16.650
Come back to 2014,
we had 56 petabytes.

00:10:16.650 --> 00:10:19.680
It is 300% increase.

00:10:19.680 --> 00:10:22.890
And data centers in 21
countries and more than 94,000

00:10:22.890 --> 00:10:27.320
plus servers, and some other
statistics on the slide there.

00:10:27.320 --> 00:10:30.550
So going to the cloud
journey, so HSBC

00:10:30.550 --> 00:10:34.380
has started the cloud
journey with Google about 2

00:10:34.380 --> 00:10:36.160
and 1/2 to 3 years back.

00:10:36.160 --> 00:10:39.580
In 2017, we started our
development environments.

00:10:39.580 --> 00:10:43.540
And we started working on
the different requirements,

00:10:43.540 --> 00:10:45.070
understanding
Google capabilities,

00:10:45.070 --> 00:10:47.930
and mapping that to other needs.

00:10:47.930 --> 00:10:52.090
And by the end of 2017, we
had a contract with Google.

00:10:52.090 --> 00:10:55.360
And Google was selected
as a strategic partner

00:10:55.360 --> 00:10:58.710
for data analytics services.

00:10:58.710 --> 00:11:02.620
Going ahead in 2018, we started
building our Google Cloud

00:11:02.620 --> 00:11:04.160
foundation platform.

00:11:04.160 --> 00:11:08.350
And HSBC, being a very
highly regulated industry,

00:11:08.350 --> 00:11:10.150
we need to make
sure that we adhere

00:11:10.150 --> 00:11:13.660
to all the regulatory
policies and also make sure

00:11:13.660 --> 00:11:17.800
that our data is secured,
addressed, in motion,

00:11:17.800 --> 00:11:21.280
and also we have the
complete control of data.

00:11:21.280 --> 00:11:23.770
So in this regard, we
worked with Google,

00:11:23.770 --> 00:11:26.290
collaborated with them,
coordinated with them.

00:11:26.290 --> 00:11:28.630
And as Chad said, some
of the features you

00:11:28.630 --> 00:11:31.630
have seen in the previous
slides were implemented for us

00:11:31.630 --> 00:11:34.330
to make sure that
your data is secured

00:11:34.330 --> 00:11:38.650
and we don't have any data
leakages, data exfiltrations.

00:11:38.650 --> 00:11:40.240
So those services
are the customer

00:11:40.240 --> 00:11:43.930
managed encryption key,
the VPC Service Controls,

00:11:43.930 --> 00:11:46.180
and access transparency logs.

00:11:46.180 --> 00:11:48.580
These were really
helping us to make sure

00:11:48.580 --> 00:11:52.060
that we have complete security
and we don't have any data

00:11:52.060 --> 00:11:53.950
leakage or data exfiltration.

00:11:53.950 --> 00:11:57.640
And it also helped us to
get the approvals to move

00:11:57.640 --> 00:12:00.640
sensitive data to Google Cloud.

00:12:00.640 --> 00:12:04.300
In parallel, in 2018, we
started analyzing our database

00:12:04.300 --> 00:12:08.640
warehouses to see which
of them can move to cloud.

00:12:08.640 --> 00:12:12.610
And in this slide,
you can see this

00:12:12.610 --> 00:12:17.040
is one of our critical data
analytic workloads, which were

00:12:17.040 --> 00:12:19.140
currently migrated to cloud.

00:12:19.140 --> 00:12:21.990
As you can see, we being a
big international company,

00:12:21.990 --> 00:12:24.285
we have this data
warehouse present

00:12:24.285 --> 00:12:25.920
in more than one country.

00:12:25.920 --> 00:12:28.210
We have at least
10 countries here.

00:12:28.210 --> 00:12:31.890
So our approach is that we would
like to-- we started with UK.

00:12:31.890 --> 00:12:34.770
And that is the most
complex data warehouse.

00:12:34.770 --> 00:12:38.260
And we have created
a template for that.

00:12:38.260 --> 00:12:41.030
And we'll use this template for
migration of other countries.

00:12:41.030 --> 00:12:42.810
Variable-- the
regulators allow us

00:12:42.810 --> 00:12:45.510
and Google Cloud
is present there.

00:12:45.510 --> 00:12:47.160
So what are the
primary challenges

00:12:47.160 --> 00:12:49.650
we are trying to address here?

00:12:49.650 --> 00:12:51.950
So as many of the
large organizations,

00:12:51.950 --> 00:12:55.670
HSBC has a rapid and
tremendous growth of data.

00:12:55.670 --> 00:12:58.460
And one of the constraints
we're seeing is capacity.

00:12:58.460 --> 00:13:01.830
On prem, we are
notable to have--

00:13:01.830 --> 00:13:04.760
either you have to
increase our infrastructure

00:13:04.760 --> 00:13:07.900
to meet all the data
coming in and to meet

00:13:07.900 --> 00:13:11.400
our analytical needs, or we
need to look at other options.

00:13:11.400 --> 00:13:13.830
And of course, that's the cloud.

00:13:13.830 --> 00:13:16.820
Secondly, our business
is really constrained,

00:13:16.820 --> 00:13:19.810
because I talked about
169 petabytes of data.

00:13:19.810 --> 00:13:21.560
But what is these whole
data if you're not

00:13:21.560 --> 00:13:26.120
able to get the business the
valuable insights they want?

00:13:26.120 --> 00:13:28.270
So because of capacity and
processing constraints,

00:13:28.270 --> 00:13:32.610
we are unable to really service
our customers as we want to.

00:13:32.610 --> 00:13:35.200
Another challenge is that
these warehouses have

00:13:35.200 --> 00:13:37.370
been built over
15 years of time,

00:13:37.370 --> 00:13:40.850
and we have challenges to the
metadata with data lineage.

00:13:40.850 --> 00:13:43.280
And they're causing
a duplication of data

00:13:43.280 --> 00:13:46.410
as less data quality issues.

00:13:46.410 --> 00:13:50.950
So to address these
particular challenges,

00:13:50.950 --> 00:13:53.890
we started with
journey to cloud.

00:13:53.890 --> 00:13:58.570
So the first use case I've taken
is a UK data analytic workload.

00:13:58.570 --> 00:14:00.520
So here you can see the--

00:14:00.520 --> 00:14:03.495
when I say "data warehouse,"
it's just not a simple data

00:14:03.495 --> 00:14:04.870
warehouse, but
it's a combination

00:14:04.870 --> 00:14:08.170
of-- it's an ecosystem
of the ETL jobs,

00:14:08.170 --> 00:14:11.230
reporting jobs, and
scheduling jobs,

00:14:11.230 --> 00:14:14.920
and analytical users using this
data for multiple purposes.

00:14:14.920 --> 00:14:16.350
It's not a simple thing.

00:14:16.350 --> 00:14:19.240
Just to give some
numbers to you here,

00:14:19.240 --> 00:14:21.880
this particular UK
data analytic workload

00:14:21.880 --> 00:14:28.240
has more than 6,500 ETL jobs
and more than 2,500 reports.

00:14:28.240 --> 00:14:31.760
It gets data from
about 100 sources.

00:14:31.760 --> 00:14:34.570
It has 180 terabytes of
data and a rich history

00:14:34.570 --> 00:14:38.470
of 30 years with millions of
customers and transactions.

00:14:38.470 --> 00:14:40.570
And also, it
supports regular data

00:14:40.570 --> 00:14:43.350
reporting, like liquidity
reporting and the [INAUDIBLE]

00:14:43.350 --> 00:14:44.960
reporting.

00:14:44.960 --> 00:14:49.600
So this is a huge and a
very complex critical data

00:14:49.600 --> 00:14:51.310
warehouse for HSBC.

00:14:51.310 --> 00:14:53.230
And when we embarked
on this journey

00:14:53.230 --> 00:14:58.270
of moving this to cloud, we
seeked the help of Google.

00:14:58.270 --> 00:15:02.940
So Google partnered with us
to evaluate multiple options.

00:15:02.940 --> 00:15:07.870
So evaluated options like
re-engineering, Lift and Shift.

00:15:07.870 --> 00:15:09.700
And finally, we
zeroed in on what

00:15:09.700 --> 00:15:13.220
we call as Move and Improve.

00:15:13.220 --> 00:15:15.830
So what do you mean
by "Move and Improve"?

00:15:15.830 --> 00:15:19.540
So we want to move our
data warehouse to BigQuery,

00:15:19.540 --> 00:15:24.040
get all the capabilities,
and all the capacity,

00:15:24.040 --> 00:15:26.470
and all the elasticity,
et cetera, of BigQuery.

00:15:26.470 --> 00:15:29.192
And that helps us to solve
the capacity constraints.

00:15:29.192 --> 00:15:31.150
That's what we are trying
to solve immediately,

00:15:31.150 --> 00:15:34.780
because that's where we need to
either invest more in on-prem

00:15:34.780 --> 00:15:36.840
or go to Google Cloud.

00:15:36.840 --> 00:15:40.322
And to improve on that, we
have to improve our challenges.

00:15:40.322 --> 00:15:42.030
So I talked about some
of the challenges,

00:15:42.030 --> 00:15:43.860
like metadata and data lineage.

00:15:43.860 --> 00:15:46.830
So now we are taking
this as our next task

00:15:46.830 --> 00:15:48.790
and adding metadata,
data lineage,

00:15:48.790 --> 00:15:53.370
et cetera, to our warehouse
on Google as a BigQuery

00:15:53.370 --> 00:15:55.890
and making it more simple.

00:15:55.890 --> 00:15:58.380
As far as the ETL jobs are
concerned and reporting jobs

00:15:58.380 --> 00:16:00.950
are concerned, we talked
about the numbers there,

00:16:00.950 --> 00:16:04.390
the staggering numbers
of 6,500-plus and so on.

00:16:04.390 --> 00:16:08.730
So we took a conscious decision
that the primary concern

00:16:08.730 --> 00:16:11.130
or challenge we're trying
to solve is capacity.

00:16:11.130 --> 00:16:14.820
So we'll go ahead with
the infrastructure service

00:16:14.820 --> 00:16:18.270
option for our ETL jobs
and our reporting jobs,

00:16:18.270 --> 00:16:23.880
and take over ETL and
reporting as is to Google.

00:16:23.880 --> 00:16:27.210
And also, as we are
taking this journey,

00:16:27.210 --> 00:16:29.040
we've looked at,
how can we simplify

00:16:29.040 --> 00:16:31.800
this particular
complete migration?

00:16:31.800 --> 00:16:35.400
So what we did is because
this data warehouse was built

00:16:35.400 --> 00:16:37.830
over the last 15 years,
believe it or not,

00:16:37.830 --> 00:16:39.720
we don't have a good
inventory of it.

00:16:39.720 --> 00:16:42.660
So we started looking at what
applications, what reports

00:16:42.660 --> 00:16:44.490
exist in this data
warehouse and how

00:16:44.490 --> 00:16:47.520
we can actually look
at which of them are

00:16:47.520 --> 00:16:49.110
used and unused, right?

00:16:49.110 --> 00:16:51.000
So we worked with
the stakeholders

00:16:51.000 --> 00:16:53.580
and identified about
600-plus reports

00:16:53.580 --> 00:16:55.570
are not being used at all.

00:16:55.570 --> 00:16:58.098
So we immediately said,
this can be demised.

00:16:58.098 --> 00:17:00.390
Similarly, we're working with
stakeholders and business

00:17:00.390 --> 00:17:02.970
stakeholders to understand
which of the applications

00:17:02.970 --> 00:17:08.280
are not used and which can
be not migrated, and looked

00:17:08.280 --> 00:17:09.609
at demising of those.

00:17:09.609 --> 00:17:11.280
So, in that way, we
are only migrating

00:17:11.280 --> 00:17:14.380
the applications and reports
which we want to migrate.

00:17:14.380 --> 00:17:15.839
And in this journey,
we were also

00:17:15.839 --> 00:17:18.750
looking at simplifying
our ETL jobs

00:17:18.750 --> 00:17:21.810
and taking off the technical
data of previous migrations

00:17:21.810 --> 00:17:25.920
so that production support can
have a bit of sleep at night.

00:17:25.920 --> 00:17:27.609
Coming to the core data first.

00:17:27.609 --> 00:17:30.620
So we took the approach
of moving the core data,

00:17:30.620 --> 00:17:32.380
core tables first.

00:17:32.380 --> 00:17:34.470
This gives us the
data foundation,

00:17:34.470 --> 00:17:37.920
because once we move core
data and tables on Google,

00:17:37.920 --> 00:17:40.950
we give opportunity for
the new use cases coming.

00:17:40.950 --> 00:17:45.030
And we use this data
developing Google Native.

00:17:45.030 --> 00:17:50.310
And also, we are looking
at Agile and fail fast.

00:17:50.310 --> 00:17:52.380
So we are in this
transformation journey.

00:17:52.380 --> 00:17:54.990
HSBC has been in this
transformation journey of Agile

00:17:54.990 --> 00:17:56.580
and DevOps for some time now.

00:17:56.580 --> 00:17:59.340
And this project is
completely Agile and DevOps.

00:17:59.340 --> 00:18:01.110
So we have always
looked at how we

00:18:01.110 --> 00:18:05.670
can deliver smaller workloads
and not the bigger ones,

00:18:05.670 --> 00:18:07.718
and how we can actually
use a fail fast,

00:18:07.718 --> 00:18:09.510
so that we can actually
build some patterns

00:18:09.510 --> 00:18:11.850
and proof-of-concepts and
achieve the target that we

00:18:11.850 --> 00:18:13.077
want.

00:18:13.077 --> 00:18:14.910
And one important thing
I want to talk about

00:18:14.910 --> 00:18:17.160
is innovation and automation.

00:18:17.160 --> 00:18:21.580
From day one of our journey,
we took this very seriously.

00:18:21.580 --> 00:18:24.760
We wanted to make sure
we automate everything.

00:18:24.760 --> 00:18:27.830
I mean, automate
everything possible.

00:18:27.830 --> 00:18:30.120
This is very much required,
because we are looking

00:18:30.120 --> 00:18:32.290
at a massive migration here.

00:18:32.290 --> 00:18:33.960
And if you go with
a manual effort,

00:18:33.960 --> 00:18:36.300
we'll not be able to
meet our timelines.

00:18:36.300 --> 00:18:39.540
And as any other project, we
have strict timelines to meet,

00:18:39.540 --> 00:18:43.440
because otherwise, we have
to buy new infrastructure

00:18:43.440 --> 00:18:46.420
or renew new licenses,
which you don't want to do.

00:18:46.420 --> 00:18:51.180
So with this mantra
of automation,

00:18:51.180 --> 00:18:54.390
we went ahead and created
multiple accelerators.

00:18:54.390 --> 00:18:57.360
And some of these accelerators
are the data reconciliation

00:18:57.360 --> 00:19:01.110
tool which reconciles between
the source and the target,

00:19:01.110 --> 00:19:03.360
using hash algorithm
at record level

00:19:03.360 --> 00:19:06.830
and make sure that the payload
is delivered from social target

00:19:06.830 --> 00:19:08.400
without any loss.

00:19:08.400 --> 00:19:09.970
And where there
is a discrepancy,

00:19:09.970 --> 00:19:12.240
we can actually
look at reporting,

00:19:12.240 --> 00:19:15.050
and production
support can fix that.

00:19:15.050 --> 00:19:17.730
We have one more accelator
called a SQL Parser.

00:19:17.730 --> 00:19:22.190
So many of you know that
when we look at a Native data

00:19:22.190 --> 00:19:24.230
warehouse, we have Native SQLs.

00:19:24.230 --> 00:19:26.660
And they are not one or
two, but thousands of them.

00:19:26.660 --> 00:19:29.007
In our case, there
were 3,500 SQLs.

00:19:29.007 --> 00:19:30.840
So we don't want to
migrate them one by one.

00:19:30.840 --> 00:19:33.410
So we did a automatic
parser, which

00:19:33.410 --> 00:19:40.130
migrates the SQLs of Native
into BigQuery-compatible SQLs.

00:19:40.130 --> 00:19:41.910
Third one is Data Lineage tool.

00:19:41.910 --> 00:19:44.567
This is a very critical tool
for us, because as many of you

00:19:44.567 --> 00:19:47.122
know, these warehouses have
been developed for the last 15

00:19:47.122 --> 00:19:48.580
years, so there is
no data lineage,

00:19:48.580 --> 00:19:50.070
there is no documentation.

00:19:50.070 --> 00:19:54.320
So this tool helps us to have
a lineage between the reporting

00:19:54.320 --> 00:19:57.320
to the data warehouse
to the source.

00:19:57.320 --> 00:19:59.630
So it helps us to
prioritize our workloads.

00:19:59.630 --> 00:20:01.490
As we are running
our sprints, we

00:20:01.490 --> 00:20:04.110
want to migrate, say,
x number of reports.

00:20:04.110 --> 00:20:07.250
We know which data, which tables
have been migrated before we

00:20:07.250 --> 00:20:08.390
can order the reports.

00:20:08.390 --> 00:20:12.690
And it's also useful for
our stakeholder management.

00:20:12.690 --> 00:20:15.650
And as I told you,
the Google partnership

00:20:15.650 --> 00:20:18.690
has been a great
help for us in this.

00:20:18.690 --> 00:20:24.530
So as we did the transition
of moving our ETL jobs

00:20:24.530 --> 00:20:26.750
and reporting as an
infrastructure-as-service

00:20:26.750 --> 00:20:28.490
to Google, I think
this is the first time

00:20:28.490 --> 00:20:30.510
anyone is doing that
in the entire world.

00:20:30.510 --> 00:20:32.690
So we faced challenges,
many technical challenges

00:20:32.690 --> 00:20:33.980
which we had to resolve.

00:20:33.980 --> 00:20:35.660
And that's where we
talked to Google.

00:20:35.660 --> 00:20:39.770
And Google partnered with
us in not only helping

00:20:39.770 --> 00:20:42.740
us to understand the
challenges, but also

00:20:42.740 --> 00:20:45.710
helping us to do
proof-of-concepts and building

00:20:45.710 --> 00:20:46.840
patterns.

00:20:46.840 --> 00:20:48.590
And these patterns are
very useful for us,

00:20:48.590 --> 00:20:51.230
because these helped
us to really put

00:20:51.230 --> 00:20:54.110
a factory migration in place
and not really a one-by-one.

00:20:54.110 --> 00:20:55.850
But we have a
factory running now.

00:20:55.850 --> 00:20:59.870
We actually migrated the
ETLs by bunches and by chunks

00:20:59.870 --> 00:21:02.840
depending on a
workload of migration.

00:21:02.840 --> 00:21:05.120
So, at this point
of time, I would

00:21:05.120 --> 00:21:08.758
like to invite Jan Peuker,
who is a Google PSO Lead

00:21:08.758 --> 00:21:10.550
and has been with us
for the last one year,

00:21:10.550 --> 00:21:12.040
helping us [INAUDIBLE]
accelerators

00:21:12.040 --> 00:21:13.490
with this automation.

00:21:13.490 --> 00:21:17.210
And my colleague Santa Karmakar,
who is my Senior Tech Lead,

00:21:17.210 --> 00:21:20.150
Technical Architect
at HSBC to take us

00:21:20.150 --> 00:21:22.700
a little deeper into
these accelerators

00:21:22.700 --> 00:21:24.360
and technical challenges.

00:21:24.360 --> 00:21:28.460
Santa and Jan?

00:21:28.460 --> 00:21:30.100
JAN PEUKER: Thank
you very much, Srini.

00:21:30.100 --> 00:21:32.350
Even though I've heard those
numbers many, many times,

00:21:32.350 --> 00:21:33.725
it's still
impressive, especially

00:21:33.725 --> 00:21:35.430
on such a big screen.

00:21:35.430 --> 00:21:38.420
So yeah, hi, everyone.

00:21:38.420 --> 00:21:39.190
My name's Jan.

00:21:39.190 --> 00:21:42.580
I'm a Strategic Cloud Engineer
in the Professional Services

00:21:42.580 --> 00:21:46.000
Organization of
Google in Singapore.

00:21:46.000 --> 00:21:48.310
Professional Services
helps our customers

00:21:48.310 --> 00:21:51.130
with the biggest
and most complicated

00:21:51.130 --> 00:21:53.175
technical problems,
usually through consulting,

00:21:53.175 --> 00:21:57.520
and engineering, and very
often together with partners.

00:21:57.520 --> 00:22:00.790
So before I go into
the details, let

00:22:00.790 --> 00:22:03.040
me quickly explain how
those kind of data warehouse

00:22:03.040 --> 00:22:04.900
migrations typically work.

00:22:04.900 --> 00:22:07.420
So there's two kind
of types of customers.

00:22:07.420 --> 00:22:11.860
There's the ones who just want
zero impact on their operations

00:22:11.860 --> 00:22:14.020
and they ideally want
some automated tool

00:22:14.020 --> 00:22:16.060
and just want to have
that done for them,

00:22:16.060 --> 00:22:17.530
even if it's in an
opinionated way.

00:22:17.530 --> 00:22:19.650
That's, for instance, what Chad
introduced with the BigQuery

00:22:19.650 --> 00:22:20.650
Data Migration Services.

00:22:20.650 --> 00:22:22.040
That's great.

00:22:22.040 --> 00:22:24.010
And then there's the
other type of customers

00:22:24.010 --> 00:22:26.260
who want to leverage as much
as possible in the cloud,

00:22:26.260 --> 00:22:28.376
even if it means re-engineering.

00:22:28.376 --> 00:22:31.100
And that's what Srini called
the Cloud Native approach.

00:22:31.100 --> 00:22:34.010
Now, as we heard, HSBC
is right in the middle,

00:22:34.010 --> 00:22:36.160
and that's why they engaged
Professional Services.

00:22:36.160 --> 00:22:37.868
Because in the Move
and Improve approach,

00:22:37.868 --> 00:22:40.670
we need to break that
down a little bit more.

00:22:40.670 --> 00:22:43.440
So in order to visualize
that a little bit,

00:22:43.440 --> 00:22:45.280
I'm going to show you
a classic reference

00:22:45.280 --> 00:22:47.590
architecture very briefly
for data warehouses.

00:22:47.590 --> 00:22:48.760
So those three boxes--

00:22:48.760 --> 00:22:52.228
boxes are always great, boxes
mean architecture diagram.

00:22:52.228 --> 00:22:54.520
On the left, we have, for
instance, an ingestion layer;

00:22:54.520 --> 00:22:56.650
in the middle, you have
processing and staging;

00:22:56.650 --> 00:23:00.010
and on the right, you have
your reporting and analytics,

00:23:00.010 --> 00:23:01.080
normally.

00:23:01.080 --> 00:23:03.280
So if we would go in
a standard project,

00:23:03.280 --> 00:23:06.400
we would typically suggest one
of our reference architectures,

00:23:06.400 --> 00:23:10.010
like a reference architecture
for ETL, which look like this.

00:23:10.010 --> 00:23:11.920
You have your batch
files on cloud storage.

00:23:11.920 --> 00:23:15.400
You get some events and
streaming through Pub/Sub.

00:23:15.400 --> 00:23:18.850
You process that in Cloud
Dataflow for your ETL.

00:23:18.850 --> 00:23:22.030
But maybe you're not
completely event-based,

00:23:22.030 --> 00:23:23.560
so you still need
some scheduling

00:23:23.560 --> 00:23:26.080
and some dependency
management in place.

00:23:26.080 --> 00:23:29.140
Maybe you even do
ETL inside, so you

00:23:29.140 --> 00:23:30.430
use Cloud Composer for that.

00:23:30.430 --> 00:23:33.070
And all of that goes into our
petabyte-scale, server-less

00:23:33.070 --> 00:23:35.271
data warehouse BigQuery.

00:23:35.271 --> 00:23:38.680
Now, as Srini said before,
we couldn't just use that.

00:23:38.680 --> 00:23:41.590
And Santa is going to walk us a
little bit through the details

00:23:41.590 --> 00:23:44.230
of HSBC's more
complicated architecture

00:23:44.230 --> 00:23:46.780
and what exactly
we did to resolve.

00:23:46.780 --> 00:23:49.030
SANTA DAS KARMAKAR:
Thank you, Jan.

00:23:49.030 --> 00:23:50.060
Hello, everyone.

00:23:50.060 --> 00:23:51.940
My name is Santa Das Karmakar.

00:23:51.940 --> 00:23:55.270
I'm a Senior Lead
Architect in HSBC India.

00:23:55.270 --> 00:23:57.300
I'm really privileged
and honored to share

00:23:57.300 --> 00:24:02.390
a few of my experiences in this
journey with you all today.

00:24:02.390 --> 00:24:04.860
The current platform
is pretty complex.

00:24:04.860 --> 00:24:07.640
It has numerous types
of free to be processed.

00:24:07.640 --> 00:24:11.630
N number of tools are in use
and many different complex

00:24:11.630 --> 00:24:15.110
interfaces with various
trusted source systems.

00:24:15.110 --> 00:24:19.420
As Jan said, it
has three layers--

00:24:19.420 --> 00:24:23.010
ingestion layer, processing
layer, and reporting layer.

00:24:23.010 --> 00:24:26.290
Once the various feed from
the trusted source systems

00:24:26.290 --> 00:24:29.830
arrive at the staging
areas, the scheduler at work

00:24:29.830 --> 00:24:31.570
invokes a self-script job.

00:24:31.570 --> 00:24:36.160
It first inserts a record
into a job control list table.

00:24:36.160 --> 00:24:40.270
This table has a lot of
inserts and update operations.

00:24:40.270 --> 00:24:44.710
After that, ETL tool performs a
lot of complex transformations

00:24:44.710 --> 00:24:45.760
following business rules.

00:24:45.760 --> 00:24:50.020
A few such transformations
like deduplication of records,

00:24:50.020 --> 00:24:52.720
then historical data processing
based on slowly changing

00:24:52.720 --> 00:24:57.310
dimension, snapshot load, key
generations, key lookup, et

00:24:57.310 --> 00:24:58.210
cetera.

00:24:58.210 --> 00:25:02.360
At the end of the processing,
it generates a load-ready file.

00:25:02.360 --> 00:25:06.890
This file gets inserted into
our system via various DB

00:25:06.890 --> 00:25:08.170
utilities.

00:25:08.170 --> 00:25:10.510
We first create the
core warehouse table,

00:25:10.510 --> 00:25:14.050
which comprises the 360-degree
view of the customer.

00:25:14.050 --> 00:25:17.570
After that, various
aggregation tables

00:25:17.570 --> 00:25:19.630
forms the semantic layer.

00:25:19.630 --> 00:25:22.220
Since the current architecture
is pretty complex,

00:25:22.220 --> 00:25:26.041
we could not use the
reference architecture as is.

00:25:26.041 --> 00:25:26.833
JAN PEUKER: Thanks.

00:25:26.833 --> 00:25:28.680
Thank you.

00:25:28.680 --> 00:25:30.380
Thank you, Santa.

00:25:30.380 --> 00:25:32.710
So if you have such
a complicated system,

00:25:32.710 --> 00:25:35.410
what you need to do is
break down your work.

00:25:35.410 --> 00:25:38.260
Now, again, there's two options
to break down your work.

00:25:38.260 --> 00:25:40.900
The classical enterprise
architecture approaches

00:25:40.900 --> 00:25:45.157
would be either to go system
by system or data by data.

00:25:45.157 --> 00:25:46.990
System by system, meaning
you go through all

00:25:46.990 --> 00:25:48.430
of your dependent
systems, and you just

00:25:48.430 --> 00:25:49.980
check which ones
have to be migrated,

00:25:49.980 --> 00:25:51.640
and you basically
make a list of all

00:25:51.640 --> 00:25:55.000
of your dependent applications
and of your sources.

00:25:55.000 --> 00:25:56.950
And data-driven approach
is a little bit more.

00:25:56.950 --> 00:25:59.030
You go into the
schemas themselves,

00:25:59.030 --> 00:26:01.370
and then you find out
which schemas are used,

00:26:01.370 --> 00:26:04.800
and then you migrate data
by data incrementally.

00:26:04.800 --> 00:26:09.080
Now, in this particular
project, as Srini said before,

00:26:09.080 --> 00:26:11.362
we decided, actually,
against those two approaches,

00:26:11.362 --> 00:26:13.070
because we wanted to
have a real business

00:26:13.070 --> 00:26:15.180
priority and a business impact.

00:26:15.180 --> 00:26:19.100
So rather, we decided to go with
architecture decision records.

00:26:19.100 --> 00:26:22.172
And those would be based
on technical user journeys.

00:26:22.172 --> 00:26:24.380
So in order to develop those
technical user journeys,

00:26:24.380 --> 00:26:26.083
we basically did an Agile board.

00:26:26.083 --> 00:26:27.500
I guess all of you
have seen this.

00:26:27.500 --> 00:26:29.480
This basically looks
like a scrum board.

00:26:29.480 --> 00:26:32.370
You can imagine those
to be user stories.

00:26:32.370 --> 00:26:35.570
So I'm going to give you a few
examples of those user stories.

00:26:35.570 --> 00:26:38.630
Ours that we used were much,
much further broken down,

00:26:38.630 --> 00:26:41.660
but for the ones who know data
warehouses, some of those words

00:26:41.660 --> 00:26:43.580
might already make sense to you.

00:26:43.580 --> 00:26:46.350
We have mentioned, for instance,
Change Data Capture, Product

00:26:46.350 --> 00:26:47.780
Event Handling.

00:26:47.780 --> 00:26:49.730
We had Slowly
Changing Dimensions,

00:26:49.730 --> 00:26:56.330
which is sometimes also called
Delta Load or Incremental Load.

00:26:56.330 --> 00:26:58.220
Those are the typical
data warehouse topics

00:26:58.220 --> 00:26:59.720
that you just need
to address if you

00:26:59.720 --> 00:27:01.873
go through such a migration.

00:27:01.873 --> 00:27:03.290
But we had some
other topics which

00:27:03.290 --> 00:27:06.620
were more specific to the
financial services industry.

00:27:06.620 --> 00:27:09.290
Santa briefly spoke
about deduplication

00:27:09.290 --> 00:27:11.690
to make sure that the data
warehouse has a very, very

00:27:11.690 --> 00:27:16.460
consistent golden source of
data at a specific point in time

00:27:16.460 --> 00:27:18.760
that you need to maintain.

00:27:18.760 --> 00:27:22.570
But our impact framework didn't
only base on the migration

00:27:22.570 --> 00:27:23.810
impact.

00:27:23.810 --> 00:27:26.590
For instance, we also looked
at the business impacts.

00:27:26.590 --> 00:27:29.830
We prioritized the archival
data and historic data,

00:27:29.830 --> 00:27:32.830
because that allowed us to move
already things into the cloud

00:27:32.830 --> 00:27:35.110
and, therefore, take
load off the old system,

00:27:35.110 --> 00:27:38.590
making the old system
faster for the users as well

00:27:38.590 --> 00:27:40.810
as the new system.

00:27:40.810 --> 00:27:43.870
Another impact was a cultural
and organizational impact.

00:27:43.870 --> 00:27:46.750
Something as trivial
as SQL status codes

00:27:46.750 --> 00:27:49.420
can be very important for
your production support teams.

00:27:49.420 --> 00:27:51.430
So we made sure that
everyone knows them,

00:27:51.430 --> 00:27:54.130
that we get the right metrics,
the right observability

00:27:54.130 --> 00:27:56.200
very early on in the
process to also start

00:27:56.200 --> 00:27:58.870
to explain to people that they
don't have to manage hardware

00:27:58.870 --> 00:28:01.060
anymore, but now have
to manage queries,

00:28:01.060 --> 00:28:03.592
and slots, and
performance, and quotas,

00:28:03.592 --> 00:28:05.050
and look at very
different metrics.

00:28:05.050 --> 00:28:08.410
That's changed, as it
takes humans a longer time.

00:28:08.410 --> 00:28:12.820
So we wanted to get that started
very early in the process.

00:28:12.820 --> 00:28:16.590
So, once we had those technical
user journeys defined,

00:28:16.590 --> 00:28:20.640
we broke them further
down into POCs

00:28:20.640 --> 00:28:24.360
and developed different
options for those POCs.

00:28:24.360 --> 00:28:27.300
In those POCs, we looked
again at the principles

00:28:27.300 --> 00:28:30.450
that Srini mentioned before,
but now broke them down

00:28:30.450 --> 00:28:32.948
a little bit further into
technical principles.

00:28:32.948 --> 00:28:34.740
And Santa's going to
talk about them again.

00:28:37.615 --> 00:28:39.490
SANTA DAS KARMAKAR: Once
the various patterns

00:28:39.490 --> 00:28:44.750
are identified, we establish
a few key design principles,

00:28:44.750 --> 00:28:49.190
which we followed throughout
the execution of the project.

00:28:49.190 --> 00:28:53.120
The current platform is aging,
and it has a lot of disruption.

00:28:53.120 --> 00:28:57.530
With the use of various past
services such as Google Cloud

00:28:57.530 --> 00:29:01.970
Storage for staging area
and BigQuery for warehouse,

00:29:01.970 --> 00:29:06.020
we wanted to improve the
performance of the system.

00:29:06.020 --> 00:29:09.050
We wanted to maintain only
a single copy of the data.

00:29:09.050 --> 00:29:15.200
By restricting multiple copies,
we improved the maintenance

00:29:15.200 --> 00:29:19.130
overhead, and also,
we reduced the cost.

00:29:19.130 --> 00:29:22.430
The data is always secured with
customer-managed encryption

00:29:22.430 --> 00:29:29.440
keys, and we also applied BBC
Service Control to prevent

00:29:29.440 --> 00:29:31.750
the data exfiltrations.

00:29:31.750 --> 00:29:33.970
Our consumption layer
and processing layer

00:29:33.970 --> 00:29:35.680
are separated out.

00:29:35.680 --> 00:29:38.140
We have used multiple
authorized views

00:29:38.140 --> 00:29:40.840
to give access to the
data to various reporting

00:29:40.840 --> 00:29:44.260
layers and analytical users.

00:29:44.260 --> 00:29:48.790
With this, let me take you to
our data migration strategy,

00:29:48.790 --> 00:29:49.960
or steps.

00:29:49.960 --> 00:29:52.130
It has three steps.

00:29:52.130 --> 00:29:56.140
Number 1, migrate the
AS-IS Schema onto BigQuery

00:29:56.140 --> 00:29:59.730
and re-point all the
analytical and reporting load

00:29:59.730 --> 00:30:02.060
onto BigQuery.

00:30:02.060 --> 00:30:08.460
Step number 2, migrate all our
historical data onto BigQuery.

00:30:08.460 --> 00:30:12.930
Part of the second step is
set up a change data process,

00:30:12.930 --> 00:30:18.820
so that the warehouse system and
BigQuery always remain in sync.

00:30:18.820 --> 00:30:21.510
Set up a data
reconciliation tool

00:30:21.510 --> 00:30:26.730
and use metadata tagging as or
where possible and applicable.

00:30:26.730 --> 00:30:30.600
Third step, migrate
the data access layer

00:30:30.600 --> 00:30:33.690
by converting all
the SQL script into

00:30:33.690 --> 00:30:36.420
BigQuery-compliant scripts.

00:30:36.420 --> 00:30:41.430
This seems to be a very, very
long and time-consuming task.

00:30:41.430 --> 00:30:44.610
To speed up the development
and reduce the human error,

00:30:44.610 --> 00:30:47.430
we introduced a lot
of accelerators.

00:30:47.430 --> 00:30:51.300
Here we are going to talk
about four accelerators today.

00:30:51.300 --> 00:30:54.750
The first accelerator, Schema
Parser and Data Reconciliation

00:30:54.750 --> 00:30:59.010
Tool, helps us migrating our
data layer onto BigQuery.

00:30:59.010 --> 00:31:02.520
SQL Parser helps us migrating
the data access layer

00:31:02.520 --> 00:31:04.600
onto GCB platform.

00:31:04.600 --> 00:31:07.710
And Data Lineage tool
provides a dependency

00:31:07.710 --> 00:31:11.040
across various components
in the pipeline.

00:31:11.040 --> 00:31:14.810
With that, let's look
into these tools.

00:31:14.810 --> 00:31:17.700
I will combine the Schema
Parser and Data Reconciliation

00:31:17.700 --> 00:31:19.930
tool in my next slide.

00:31:19.930 --> 00:31:22.500
And here it is.

00:31:22.500 --> 00:31:25.200
From the source system,
we first extract

00:31:25.200 --> 00:31:26.970
all the table
definition information

00:31:26.970 --> 00:31:30.780
and create a Avro
Schema Parse file.

00:31:30.780 --> 00:31:34.740
And then we fetch all the
records from a specific table,

00:31:34.740 --> 00:31:36.900
and we convert them
into Avro format,

00:31:36.900 --> 00:31:38.640
load it into a data file.

00:31:38.640 --> 00:31:43.410
While doing so, we also apply
hashing perform operations

00:31:43.410 --> 00:31:46.560
and create a hash data.

00:31:46.560 --> 00:31:48.360
Load that data into
a different file

00:31:48.360 --> 00:31:50.970
along with all the
key elements in it.

00:31:50.970 --> 00:31:53.080
We call this as a Passport file.

00:31:53.080 --> 00:31:56.550
Once the hash file,
and the Passport file,

00:31:56.550 --> 00:31:59.340
the hash information, and
the data files are created,

00:31:59.340 --> 00:32:04.980
we load this into
GCS using GS util.

00:32:04.980 --> 00:32:08.310
From there, we execute
VQ load command

00:32:08.310 --> 00:32:10.680
to load the data into BigQuery.

00:32:10.680 --> 00:32:13.480
If the tables are not created,
first, you create the table.

00:32:13.480 --> 00:32:15.600
Then the data gets loaded there.

00:32:15.600 --> 00:32:18.090
Once the data is
loaded into BigQuery,

00:32:18.090 --> 00:32:20.520
we again apply the
same hashing operation,

00:32:20.520 --> 00:32:22.920
but this time, we
do it on BigQuery

00:32:22.920 --> 00:32:24.630
and get the hash value.

00:32:24.630 --> 00:32:26.370
And then we compare
the hash value

00:32:26.370 --> 00:32:28.170
with the data, what
we have received,

00:32:28.170 --> 00:32:29.900
using the Passport file.

00:32:29.900 --> 00:32:32.850
Whatever differences,
discrepancies are there,

00:32:32.850 --> 00:32:35.760
it gets reported into
our reporting layer.

00:32:35.760 --> 00:32:37.500
And our products
and support team

00:32:37.500 --> 00:32:41.910
finds it quite useful
to correct this data.

00:32:41.910 --> 00:32:44.160
This tool is quite
helpful and very quick.

00:32:44.160 --> 00:32:47.250
With the help of
this tool, we could

00:32:47.250 --> 00:32:49.380
reconcile data for
a specific table

00:32:49.380 --> 00:32:52.480
in a couple of minutes, which
generally takes almost an hour

00:32:52.480 --> 00:32:54.330
if I want to do it manually.

00:32:54.330 --> 00:32:57.090
It has consistent and
repeatable processes,

00:32:57.090 --> 00:32:59.890
which eliminates a
lot of human errors.

00:32:59.890 --> 00:33:05.260
Audit is enabled in every
operation in this tool.

00:33:05.260 --> 00:33:09.310
Moving the historical data onto
BigQuery is quite an easy task.

00:33:09.310 --> 00:33:12.700
But when we are moving the
changed data on every devices

00:33:12.700 --> 00:33:16.870
to sync my on-prem data
warehouse with BigQuery,

00:33:16.870 --> 00:33:21.040
we need to set up a change
SED type to operations.

00:33:21.040 --> 00:33:23.230
So that was a little
tricky for us.

00:33:23.230 --> 00:33:25.550
When you are migrating
a small table,

00:33:25.550 --> 00:33:27.340
it's just a one-step process.

00:33:27.340 --> 00:33:31.000
But if it is a big table, we
need to migrate the data chunk

00:33:31.000 --> 00:33:33.880
by chunk.

00:33:33.880 --> 00:33:37.820
So once we have migrated
the data layer successfully,

00:33:37.820 --> 00:33:41.615
then we look at how can we
migrate the data access layer.

00:33:41.615 --> 00:33:42.740
What would be the strategy?

00:33:42.740 --> 00:33:44.800
How can I bring a
lot of parallelism?

00:33:44.800 --> 00:33:46.510
That is what cloud is known for.

00:33:46.510 --> 00:33:49.710
For that, Jan is going
to talk about it.

00:33:49.710 --> 00:33:51.270
JAN PEUKER: Thanks a lot, Santa.

00:33:51.270 --> 00:33:56.370
So as Santa just
said, we couldn't just

00:33:56.370 --> 00:33:57.720
take the easiest approach.

00:33:57.720 --> 00:34:00.000
So once we had our
technical user journeys

00:34:00.000 --> 00:34:02.970
and we had broken
down the decisions,

00:34:02.970 --> 00:34:04.920
we could have just
done different POCs

00:34:04.920 --> 00:34:06.750
and decided for
whatever is the easiest.

00:34:06.750 --> 00:34:07.890
But the goal was--

00:34:07.890 --> 00:34:11.219
Srini's goal, really, was
still to Move and Improve.

00:34:11.219 --> 00:34:13.815
So we still wanted to get
a faster time to insight.

00:34:13.815 --> 00:34:15.690
We wanted to get high
availability and higher

00:34:15.690 --> 00:34:17.320
performance.

00:34:17.320 --> 00:34:20.159
So we couldn't just take that.

00:34:20.159 --> 00:34:21.719
And to illustrate
that, I'm quickly

00:34:21.719 --> 00:34:25.750
going to show an example based
on surrogate key generation.

00:34:25.750 --> 00:34:30.420
So in your regular relational
database or data warehouse,

00:34:30.420 --> 00:34:33.120
you generate surrogate keys
typically using a sequence

00:34:33.120 --> 00:34:35.530
or some kind of row identity.

00:34:35.530 --> 00:34:38.580
Now, we could have also
still generated a sequence,

00:34:38.580 --> 00:34:41.850
but ordering stuff manually,
monotonically increasing,

00:34:41.850 --> 00:34:43.302
there's always a bottleneck.

00:34:43.302 --> 00:34:45.219
There are some academic
solutions around that,

00:34:45.219 --> 00:34:47.250
but the fact is if you
do that somewhere, it's

00:34:47.250 --> 00:34:48.570
going to make stuff slower.

00:34:48.570 --> 00:34:50.815
Now, BigQuery is fast,
because it parallelizes.

00:34:50.815 --> 00:34:52.440
So we wanted to find
a solution that we

00:34:52.440 --> 00:34:54.719
can parallelize while
still maintaining

00:34:54.719 --> 00:34:56.790
full consistency
of our batch loads

00:34:56.790 --> 00:35:00.140
and make sure that
reconciliation is successful.

00:35:00.140 --> 00:35:01.730
So how did we do this?

00:35:01.730 --> 00:35:04.410
We did this using UUIDs.

00:35:04.410 --> 00:35:06.050
Now, I used the
word "hash" here,

00:35:06.050 --> 00:35:09.250
because we're actually not using
the UUID function in BigQuery.

00:35:09.250 --> 00:35:11.240
We're actually hashing
multiple columns

00:35:11.240 --> 00:35:13.690
that redefine as key columns.

00:35:13.690 --> 00:35:15.690
The trick here is-- there's
actually two tricks.

00:35:15.690 --> 00:35:18.570
One is we're using the
information schema in BigQuery

00:35:18.570 --> 00:35:19.830
to find those key columns.

00:35:19.830 --> 00:35:21.870
And to find the
schema itself, we

00:35:21.870 --> 00:35:24.330
worked very early in the alpha
and beta programs together

00:35:24.330 --> 00:35:27.090
to use the information
schema functionality.

00:35:27.090 --> 00:35:30.330
And the second trick was
to use a MERGE statement.

00:35:30.330 --> 00:35:32.250
Now, you would wonder,
we're just inserting,

00:35:32.250 --> 00:35:33.510
why do you want to merge?

00:35:33.510 --> 00:35:35.840
Ignoring now the history
processing itself, but

00:35:35.840 --> 00:35:38.640
if I just insert new values,
why do I need to merge?

00:35:38.640 --> 00:35:42.060
The trick here is the merge
has an update if it matches.

00:35:42.060 --> 00:35:44.530
In that update, I
can throw an error.

00:35:44.530 --> 00:35:46.860
And now I know exactly
which roles don't match.

00:35:46.860 --> 00:35:49.532
So I don't actually execute the
merge, but I throw an error,

00:35:49.532 --> 00:35:51.240
and that gives me
exactly the information

00:35:51.240 --> 00:35:52.980
of which rows and
columns are matched

00:35:52.980 --> 00:35:56.460
and which are a problem
in the actual process.

00:35:56.460 --> 00:35:58.970
Now, once we had that solution
and working very fine,

00:35:58.970 --> 00:36:02.017
we actually found that we could
also solve another problem.

00:36:02.017 --> 00:36:04.100
In some data warehouses,
you have a feature that's

00:36:04.100 --> 00:36:06.370
called Unique Set Tables.

00:36:06.370 --> 00:36:09.040
It's a rather obscure feature,
in my opinion, that allows

00:36:09.040 --> 00:36:10.720
you to basically ignore errors.

00:36:10.720 --> 00:36:13.360
You just insert into a table,
and if something is already

00:36:13.360 --> 00:36:15.160
there, it doesn't
actually insert.

00:36:15.160 --> 00:36:18.150
It basically automatically
deduplicates.

00:36:18.150 --> 00:36:20.680
We don't have that
in BigQuery, but we

00:36:20.680 --> 00:36:21.850
could use the same solution.

00:36:21.850 --> 00:36:25.210
We could just define all
columns as a row identity

00:36:25.210 --> 00:36:27.082
and then apply the
same process again.

00:36:27.082 --> 00:36:29.290
Just, in this case, it would
be the other way around.

00:36:29.290 --> 00:36:32.710
We would just not do anything
if there was already a match

00:36:32.710 --> 00:36:35.163
or insert a new record.

00:36:35.163 --> 00:36:37.330
This way, we could leverage
the full parallelization

00:36:37.330 --> 00:36:39.370
of BigQuery when
ingesting the data

00:36:39.370 --> 00:36:42.400
while still maintaining the
highest snapshot isolation

00:36:42.400 --> 00:36:46.060
level, and the full audit lock,
and the full reconciliation

00:36:46.060 --> 00:36:49.000
capability, knowing, at
each exact step, what we did

00:36:49.000 --> 00:36:50.870
and what happened.

00:36:50.870 --> 00:36:52.700
With that, I'm going
to go back to Santa,

00:36:52.700 --> 00:36:55.760
who's going to explain how we
automated that whole process

00:36:55.760 --> 00:36:57.012
inside the SQL Parser.

00:36:59.903 --> 00:37:01.361
SANTA DAS KARMAKAR:
Thank you, Jan.

00:37:04.520 --> 00:37:07.730
So BigQuery and
on-prem warehouse,

00:37:07.730 --> 00:37:11.810
they are not the same
syntactically and semantically.

00:37:11.810 --> 00:37:15.740
So existing SQL script
in current warehouse

00:37:15.740 --> 00:37:18.710
does not work as is on BigQuery.

00:37:18.710 --> 00:37:22.820
For example, delete star
from a specific table

00:37:22.820 --> 00:37:25.903
works perfectly OK in
most of the RDBMS system.

00:37:25.903 --> 00:37:27.320
But in BigQuery,
it does not work.

00:37:27.320 --> 00:37:32.870
You need to add an additional
WHERE clause at the end.

00:37:32.870 --> 00:37:39.020
Few data type such as small
int, CAD, worker, et cetera,

00:37:39.020 --> 00:37:41.740
there is no direct
mapping in BigQuery.

00:37:41.740 --> 00:37:43.750
However, good news
is in BigQuery

00:37:43.750 --> 00:37:47.360
you have similar data type
with higher precision.

00:37:47.360 --> 00:37:48.330
Example?

00:37:48.330 --> 00:37:50.930
String and n64.

00:37:50.930 --> 00:37:52.610
So there is no
data loss when you

00:37:52.610 --> 00:37:59.540
migrate the data from existing
warehouse onto BigQuery.

00:37:59.540 --> 00:38:03.740
The time table or
volatile table deconstruct

00:38:03.740 --> 00:38:07.490
are actually different in
these two environments.

00:38:07.490 --> 00:38:11.270
So SQL Parser exactly
identifies all the scenarios

00:38:11.270 --> 00:38:14.300
from the existing SQL
script and converts them

00:38:14.300 --> 00:38:17.600
into BigQuery-compliant scripts.

00:38:17.600 --> 00:38:19.580
With this script,
we could improve

00:38:19.580 --> 00:38:24.290
the performance, the development
effort by 50% to 60%.

00:38:24.290 --> 00:38:27.380
There are many such
scenarios we have identified

00:38:27.380 --> 00:38:30.890
while going during the
development phases,

00:38:30.890 --> 00:38:35.300
and we all included them
into the SQL Parser as

00:38:35.300 --> 00:38:36.560
and when we found it.

00:38:36.560 --> 00:38:38.000
It's a very quick tool.

00:38:38.000 --> 00:38:40.220
Easy and super helpful tool.

00:38:40.220 --> 00:38:42.950
With that, let me take
you to the last tool

00:38:42.950 --> 00:38:44.420
for today's discussion.

00:38:44.420 --> 00:38:46.370
That is Data Lineage.

00:38:46.370 --> 00:38:50.150
This tool is actually a scrum
master's and project manager's

00:38:50.150 --> 00:38:51.110
delight.

00:38:51.110 --> 00:38:56.900
What it does, it discovers the
components from various layers

00:38:56.900 --> 00:38:59.780
and identifies all the
dependent components,

00:38:59.780 --> 00:39:04.040
starting from the ETL feed
to ETL jobs, then warehouse

00:39:04.040 --> 00:39:05.930
tables, and packages.

00:39:05.930 --> 00:39:09.410
So scrum master
finds it quite useful

00:39:09.410 --> 00:39:12.200
to identify only the
dependent component which

00:39:12.200 --> 00:39:16.610
is required for a
specific MVP in a sprint

00:39:16.610 --> 00:39:17.720
and allocate the work.

00:39:17.720 --> 00:39:19.040
How it works?

00:39:19.040 --> 00:39:22.480
It picks up a reporting
file as an input,

00:39:22.480 --> 00:39:26.670
identifies the corresponding
tables from there,

00:39:26.670 --> 00:39:29.030
which ETL job
populates those tables,

00:39:29.030 --> 00:39:31.790
and then what are the
ETL feeds required

00:39:31.790 --> 00:39:33.640
to populate those ETL jobs.

00:39:33.640 --> 00:39:35.750
A very super helpful tool.

00:39:35.750 --> 00:39:38.240
With the help of this
tool, we could also

00:39:38.240 --> 00:39:40.610
